# hp-xubuntu.env — the per-node DATA the bare-metal rebuild procedure consumes
# for the fleet's THIRD pool node, and the first one that is a FACTORY HOST
# first and a pool member second.
#
# THIS FILE CARRIES NO PROCEDURE. It is read by the SAME ../storage-layout.sh,
# the same ../../provision-k3s.sh and the same ansible/ci-pool.yml roles the
# other two nodes' profiles are read by, through the same shared parser
# ../profile.sh. A third pool node is a third PROFILE, never a third procedure
# (SPECIFICATION/non-functional-requirements.md §"Runner-pool node rebuild
# recipe", "One profile per node").
#
# FORMAT: identical to the other two nodes', because one parser reads them all —
# `KEY=value` one per line, `#` comments and blank lines ignored, PARSED and
# never sourced, list-valued keys holding space-separated `:`-delimited records.
# Read ./poweredge-xubuntu.env's header for the format in full; a second copy of
# it would be a second thing to drift.
#
# WHAT THIS NODE IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. It joins as a FULL AGENT — every
# node-local facility that APPLIES to it is converged. From 2026-10-13 to
# 2026-11-03 it was CLOSED TO CI (maintainer rulings 2026-10-12 and 2026-10-13,
# livespec epic livespec-sqvj7p). Since 2026-11-03 it is OPEN TO CI RUNNERS
# WITHOUT CARRYING STORAGE TIERS (livespec-dev-tooling-zreev4; fleet coordinator
# ruling under R301 on the maintainer's order that hp carry CI load), said three
# ways the parser checks against each other (../profile.sh):
#   * CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only — it still carries none of the pool's CI
#     storage tiers, so it declares no physical volume, no volume group, no
#     logical volume and no tier, and the storage stage plans nothing. Its
#     runner work volumes live under RUNNER_WORKVOL_DIR on the existing
#     `sandbox-stores` mount; its jobs read the pool's host-served caches over
#     the node network and start with no seeded tree;
#   * ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=5 — it advertises five churn slots, DERIVED from
#     its 16 CPUs at the pool's one cores-per-slot ratio (120 CPUs / 44 slots,
#     about 2.73) and rounded down for its gate lane and draining sandboxes
#     (../../phase2/kueue/DERIVATION.md "Per-node churn slots derived from
#     cores (2026-11-04)", livespec-dev-tooling-qt3q). It opened at 8 on
#     2026-11-03 (the "rebalance at C = 44" section there); at 8 it ran 6 jobs
#     at PSI60 40.7 and 0% idle while poweredge and gmktec sat 33% and 54%
#     idle. The cohort total stays 44 (poweredge 27 + gmktec 12 + hp 5) and
#     cannot rise past 45 while the 450-point JIT startup budget is the only
#     cap on a cold start;
#   * NODE_TAINTS is empty — the capacity opens Kueue's ADMISSION, and only an
#     absent taint lets the SCHEDULER place the runners it admits.
# WHY NOT `full`. Opening the node as `full` would make the bare-metal stage
# create volumes on disks this host has no free space on: `/dev/sdc` is the
# sandbox swap plus the `sandbox-stores` volume holding k3s's data directory.
# That is an irreversible repartition of a live factory host. `runner-only` is a
# profile edit and an apply, and reverting it is the same edit back to `none`.
#
# WHAT IT IS INSTEAD: a Fabro FACTORY host, provisioned by ansible/fabro-hosts.yml
# and STAYING in that play's `sandbox_hosts` group while it also joins `ci_pool`.
# Two Fabro server instances and the Docker sandbox slice keep running
# throughout, and the k3s agent is sized to coexist with them — see KUBELET_ARGS
# below and the budget record it points at. FACTORY_NODE=false since 2026-11-03:
# this node no longer carries the label the kubernetes-sandbox-backend plugin
# selects factory capacity on, so new factory sandboxes land on poweredge and
# gmktec and this node's cores go to CI runners (see the posture block below).
#
# PROVENANCE of the values below: the storage, network, cluster and coexistence
# values were MEASURED read-only on 2026-10-13 and are trustworthy as written.
# TWO LINES ARE MARKED `# UNVERIFIED` and are called out where they stand; both
# are base-OS keys this node never runs a stage against.
#   Read 2026-10-13, and therefore trustworthy as written:
#     - 16 CPUs, 30 GB RAM, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS;
#     - `/dev/sda1` an ext4 root carrying NO filesystem label, `/dev/sda2` the
#       EFI system partition, `/dev/sda3` mounted at `/data`;
#     - the sandbox disk `/dev/sdc`: `sdc1` the 160 GiB `sandbox-swap` and
#       `sdc2` the 3.6 TiB `sandbox-stores` mounted at /srv/sandbox-storage;
#     - the wired interface `enp11s0` at 192.168.1.4/24 — and `wlp10s0` ALSO on
#       that subnet, which is why NODE_ADDRESS must pin the wired address;
#     - the first node's Kubernetes API at https://192.168.1.200:6443, which is
#       the address CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS names;
#     - the Docker sandbox slice (MemoryHigh 22G, MemoryMax 24G, CPUQuota 1400%)
#       and the two Fabro server units, all of which keep running.
#   NOT read, and marked where they stand: ESP_SIZE and BOOT_ENTRY_LABEL.
#
# THERE IS NO `.recorded-facts` FILE BESIDE THIS ONE, and that is a decision
# rather than an omission. The other two profiles have one because
# ../storage-layout-exit-tests.sh §E/§G pins each node's declared LAYOUT against
# a host record — and this node declares no layout at all: CI_NODE_FACILITIES is
# `runner-only`, so the procedure creates no volume and no tier here and there is
# nothing for a facts record to hold it to. The two values a facts read WOULD
# settle are the two marked `# UNVERIFIED`, and both are inert (stage 2 is not a
# stage this node runs). Write the record the day this node declares tiers
# (CI_NODE_FACILITIES=full).
#
# WHAT THIS HOST RUNS THAT NOTHING HERE MAY TOUCH. Besides the factory services
# above: `/dev/sdb` is the USB backup disk and `/dev/nvme0n1` carries Windows.
# Neither is this procedure's, both are named in PRESERVED_PARTITIONS below, and
# no key here describes either of them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_NAME=hp-xubuntu

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Storage controller and its virtual disk — this node has none.
#
# CONTROLLER_KIND=none is what makes every CONTROLLER_* and VD_* value below
# permissibly EMPTY, and what makes stage 1 skip the virtual-disk stage outright
# rather than partition a device some controller was expected to present. The
# 2026-10-13 read found plain `sd*` and `nvme*` devices and no RAID controller;
# the bare disks ARE the storage.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONTROLLER_KIND=none
CONTROLLER_CLI=
CONTROLLER_ID=
VD_ENCLOSURE=
VD_SLOTS=
VD_RAID_LEVEL=
VD_STRIP_KIB=
VD_CACHE_POLICY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Partition table — DESCRIBED AND NEVER WRITTEN on this node
#
# DISK_PLAN=free-space, as on the second node: `/dev/sda` carries the Ubuntu
# this host runs and keeps. Unlike the second node, though, stage 1 does not
# even take the free tail here, because CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only declares no
# volume group for a tail partition to become. Stage 1 against this profile
# plans NOT ONE mutating command and says so.
#
# PRESERVED_PARTITIONS IS STILL LOAD-BEARING, and more so for being the only
# storage statement this file makes. It is a STANDING REFUSAL, not a consent
# question: ../storage-layout.sh scans the arguments of every mutating command
# it is about to issue and exits non-zero if any word is one of these, and
# `--i-consent-to-destroy` naming one does NOT unlock it (../README.md §"The two
# disk plans"). So a future edit that declared a tier on the wrong device is
# refused by name rather than discovered afterwards.
#
# It names SEVEN entries: the five partitions measured on the two disks this
# profile mentions, plus the two WHOLE DEVICES that are not this procedure's at
# all. `/dev/sdb` is the USB backup disk and `/dev/nvme0n1` carries Windows;
# ansible/inventory/host_vars/hp-xubuntu.yml records that neither may ever be
# touched, and the key's own rule is "device paths no stage may write to", which
# a whole device is.
#
# `/dev/sda` ITSELF IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED. Listing it would refuse the
# `sgdisk` that cuts a tail partition out of its free space — the very step this
# node would take the day it declares tiers (CI_NODE_FACILITIES=full). The partitions
# that must survive are named; the device they live on is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
DISK_PLAN=free-space
PRESERVED_PARTITIONS=/dev/sda1 /dev/sda2 /dev/sda3 /dev/sdc1 /dev/sdc2 /dev/sdb /dev/nvme0n1
TARGET_DEVICE=/dev/sda
ESP_DEVICE=/dev/sda2
# UNVERIFIED: the EFI system partition's SIZE was not read on 2026-10-13. It is
# INERT on this node — under `free-space` the ESP is a filesystem the node
# already boots from and stage 1 never `mkfs`es it, and stage 2 is not a stage
# this node runs at all — so the value is stated because the parser requires the
# key present, and is marked rather than presented as measured. 1GiB is what a
# current Ubuntu installer makes; read it off the device before trusting it for
# anything.
ESP_SIZE=1GiB
ESP_FSTYPE=vfat
# EMPTY: the format's spelling for "carries no label" (../profile.sh, §"How this
# format spells 'no label'"), permitted under `free-space` because that plan
# DESCRIBES an existing filesystem rather than making one.
ESP_LABEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CI facilities, and the storage keys their absence empties
#
# CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only is the single key that says "this node runs CI
# runner pods but carries no tier". It empties the four storage keys below — a
# node cannot name a tier it has said it does not carry, and ../profile.sh
# REFUSES any of them carrying a value under this posture — and it is what the
# roles of ansible/ci-pool.yml gate on: storage_layout stays off, while
# container_hook, sccache_binary and cache_telemetry run, because runner and job
# pods mount what they install.
#
# RUNNER_WORKVOL_DIR is where the local-path provisioner creates this node's
# runner work volumes (its nodePathMap entry for hp-xubuntu, in
# ../../phase2/local-path-provisioner/local-path-provisioner.yaml), on the
# 3.6 TiB `sandbox-stores` ext4 volume beside k3s's data directory. ext4 has no
# reflink, so the provisioner's externals seed leaves none and the ARC hook
# copies the externals itself: cold, never broken.
#
# WHY A KEY RATHER THAN AN EMPTY ROLE_TIERS. "No tiers" read off an empty list
# is indistinguishable from a line whose value was lost in an edit, and the
# consequence of reading one as the other is a tier-carrying node silently
# built — or converged — as a bare one. Two independent statements that the
# parser checks against each other cannot fail that way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CI_NODE_FACILITIES=runner-only
RUNNER_WORKVOL_DIR=/srv/sandbox-storage/runner-workvols
PV_PARTLABEL=
VOLUME_GROUPS=
LOGICAL_VOLUMES=
ROLE_TIERS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Base operating system — AND THE BASE-OS STAGE IS NOT A STAGE THIS NODE RUNS.
#
# ../base-os-install.sh installs an operating system onto the root LOGICAL
# VOLUME its profile names. This node's root is the ext4 filesystem on the
# preserved `/dev/sda1`; keeping it is the entire reason DISK_PLAN is
# `free-space`. ROOT_LABEL below is EMPTY, and an empty root label is the one
# profile shape that stage REFUSES by name, before it derives or mounts anything
# (../profile.sh's profile_require_root_volume).
#
# The keys here are otherwise INERT. The OS_* values are stated EQUAL TO THE
# OTHER TWO NODES' so that the one value that is a fleet fact — the release the
# pool's nodes run — is the same string in all three files rather than three
# strings that can drift; `resolute` is Ubuntu 26.04, which the 2026-10-13 read
# found on this host.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
OS_DISTRIBUTION=ubuntu
OS_RELEASE=resolute
OS_ARCHITECTURE=amd64
OS_MIRROR=http://archive.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
OS_SECURITY_MIRROR=http://security.ubuntu.com/ubuntu
OS_COMPONENTS=main restricted universe multiverse
KERNEL_PACKAGE=linux-image-generic
INITRAMFS_GENERATOR=dracut
# EMPTY, and load-bearing rather than merely accurate: `/dev/sda1` carries no
# ext4 label (read 2026-10-13), and this is what stage 2 refuses on.
ROOT_LABEL=
# EMPTY: a `free-space` node gets no swap VOLUME from this procedure, so there
# is none to name. This host's swap is the 160 GiB `sandbox-swap` on `sdc1`,
# which the `sandbox_storage` role owns and no key here describes.
SWAP_LABEL=
# UNVERIFIED: `efibootmgr` was not read on 2026-10-13. Only stage 2 reads this
# key and stage 2 is not a stage this node runs, so it is inert — but the CASE
# of the string is the fact when it IS read (the stage decides "an entry
# labelled this already exists" by matching it literally against efibootmgr's
# output), so read it off the host before relying on it.
BOOT_ENTRY_LABEL=Ubuntu
# The sudo-capable admin this node has, and the account ../../provision-k3s.sh
# is run as. The same account ansible/inventory/legacy.yml names as
# `ansible_user` for this host and that the `fabro_server` role's host_vars
# names as its service account.
OPERATOR_ACCOUNT=cwoolley
OPERATOR_GROUPS=sudo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network — measured, and PINNED because it has to be.
#
# This node's wired `enp11s0` and its wireless `wlp10s0` are BOTH on
# 192.168.1.0/24. A node that registered on the wireless address would be one
# the cluster cannot reach it on, so NODE_ADDRESS states the WIRED address and
# ../../provision-k3s.sh derives `--node-ip` from it. The address is stated as
# the netplan CIDR the base-OS stage would write; the k3s stage takes the bare
# address out of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
NODE_NETWORK_INTERFACE=enp11s0
NODE_ADDRESS=192.168.1.4/24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Factory posture, k3s data placement, and the factory-worker kubelet budget
#
# FACTORY_NODE=false: this node carries NO factory node label, so the
# kubernetes-sandbox-backend plugin schedules no new factory sandbox here.
# Decided 2026-11-03 by the placement owner (orchestrator plan
# kubernetes-sandbox-backend, epic bd-ib-nmuyjc), at the request of coordinator
# legacy-coordinator-agent, in line with the maintainer's direction that the
# factory moves off hp (livespec-dev-tooling-dfixhe; plan bd-ib-fyozyd). The
# evidence: over 06:00-12:00Z this node had the fleet's worst CI slowdown (p95
# 37.5 min; time-weighted 4.86x the 11-01 baseline, against poweredge 3.48x and
# gmktec 2.59x), and its three Guaranteed-QoS 4-CPU sandboxes reserved 12 of its
# 16 cores while its 8 churn slots' runners took the contention (13:00Z read:
# PSI60 88.75, load 36.9). Moving the sandboxes moves 12 cores of reservation
# from a 16-core node onto 104 cores. Sandboxes already running here finish
# where they are (a label is a scheduling input only), and the
# factory_workspace_root and sandbox_image_warm roles simply stop being reached,
# leaving what they installed inert. REVERTING is this one line back to `true`
# plus `just ansible-apply ansible/ci-pool.yml --limit hp-xubuntu --tags
# node_labels,factory_workspace_root,sandbox_image_warm_containerd`.
# ../../provision-k3s.sh renders the label into the install command at JOIN
# time, and the `node_labels` role of ansible/ci-pool.yml reconciles it
# DECLARATIVELY on every apply — k3s applies `--node-label` at registration
# only, which is exactly how a stale taint survived a reboot on gmktec
# (livespec-dev-tooling-yapwva).
#
# NODE_DATA_DIR puts k3s's whole per-node data directory — the bundled
# containerd's image and content store above all — on the 3.6 TiB
# `sandbox-stores` volume rather than on this host's root filesystem. The other
# two nodes leave it empty and bind their containerd store onto the
# `ci-containerd` TIER instead (the `storage_layout` role); this node carries no
# tier, so the placement is the data directory itself.
#   WHY IT RIDES THE INSTALL COMMAND and not a converged config file: it is a
#   BUILD-TIME fact, like CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS and CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE beside it.
#   Moving a populated data directory is a rebuild, not a converge, so there is
#   nothing for a converged carrier to keep in step — which is what separates it
#   from NODE_TAINTS, a converge-time policy that must NOT live in the unit.
#   THE MOUNT MUST BE THERE FIRST: k3s starting before /srv/sandbox-storage is
#   mounted would create the directory on the root filesystem and the mount
#   would then HIDE it. The `k3s_config` role renders a per-node
#   `RequiresMountsFor` drop-in for k3s-agent.service from this key, which is
#   what makes that impossible rather than unlikely.
#   THE STORAGE SWEEP'S ROOT: the `storage_sweep` role's shipped sweep defaults
#   STORAGE_ROOT to /var/lib/rancher/k3s/storage, which is not where this node's
#   runner volumes live. ansible/ci-pool.yml passes RUNNER_WORKVOL_DIR to that
#   role, which renders it as the unit's STORAGE_ROOT in a drop-in, so the
#   boot-time orphan sweep clears the directory runner volumes are actually in.
#
# KUBELET_ARGS is this node's FACTORY-WORKER BUDGET: what the kubelet holds back
# for the host itself (sshd, the host's daemons, the Docker daemon that still
# serves sccache-redis, and k3s-agent) on one 16-CPU / 30 GB machine, leaving the
# rest to factory pods: about 14.5 CPU and 27Gi, which fits three factory pods
# at 4 CPU / 7630Mi each. The three records are APPENDED to the fleet's shared
# `kubelet-arg` list (which carries `max-pods=200`) by the `k3s_config` role's
# per-node drop-in, never replacing it.
#   Until 2026-11-02 these numbers were sized around hp's Docker sandbox slice
#   (24G / 1400%), which left pods 500m and ~3Gi. That slice was retired on hp
#   (PR #3078), and the reservations moved down with it.
#   THE ARITHMETIC, THE RESULTING POD-ALLOCATABLE FIGURES AND THE CONDITIONS
#   UNDER WHICH THEY ARE REVISITED ARE RECORDED ONCE, in
#   ansible/roles/k3s_config/HOST_VARS.md §"hp-xubuntu factory-worker kubelet
#   budget". Read it before changing a number here.
#   TAKES EFFECT ONLY ON THE NEXT k3s-agent START: the k3s_config role restarts
#   nothing, so follow the apply with `just ansible-apply
#   ansible/restart-k3s-agent.yml -e restart_k3s_agent_confirm=hp-xubuntu`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
FACTORY_NODE=false
NODE_DATA_DIR=/srv/sandbox-storage/k3s
KUBELET_ARGS=system-reserved=cpu=1000m,memory=2048Mi kube-reserved=cpu=500m,memory=1024Mi eviction-hard=memory.available<512Mi,nodefs.available<10%,imagefs.available<10%

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster — consumed by ../../provision-k3s.sh and by the roles of
# ansible/ci-pool.yml, which read this committed profile.
#
# CLUSTER_ROLE=agent selects BOTH the k3s install command (`agent --server …`)
# and the role plan ansible/ci-pool.yml runs; it MUST agree with the
# `cluster_role` this host's entry in ansible/inventory/legacy.yml declares, and
# the `node_extended_resource` role refuses the pair when it does not.
#
# CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE is the PATH on the node holding the token k3s reads AT RUN
# TIME to authenticate to CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS — never the token, because this
# tree carries no secret. It is a PRECONDITION, asserted and never provisioned
# (ansible/README.md §"Preconditions are asserted, not provisioned"): place it
# there (mode 0600, root:root) by the attended 1Password path before
# provisioning, and ../../provision-k3s.sh REFUSES, in `--dry-run` too, if the
# file is absent or readable beyond its owner.
#
# NODE_TAINTS is EMPTY since the runner-only opening (livespec-dev-tooling-zreev4).
# It carried `node-role/ci=factory:NoSchedule` while the node was closed to CI.
# k3s applies --node-taint at registration only, so the `node_taints` role of
# ansible/ci-pool.yml is what removes the live taint, and it does so only after
# `node_extended_resource` has stamped the node's churn slots — the node
# is opened for scheduling last. Factory sandboxes keep selecting on the
# `ci-runner.io/factory` label, which FACTORY_NODE still renders.
#
# CHURN_KUBECONFIG_FILE is the PATH on this node holding the kubeconfig the
# NODE-LOCAL churn-slot reapply timer authenticates to the API with — never the
# credential, and the second PRECONDITION this file names. It is NOT
# /etc/rancher/k3s/k3s.yaml: that is the k3s SERVER's admin file and does not
# exist on an agent. The credential is minted cluster-side by
# ../../phase2/node-status-credential/provision-node-status-credential.sh and
# delivered by the `node_status_credential` role. The timer stamps this node's
# churn slots and re-stamps them every five minutes, because kubelet does
# not persist an extended resource set through the status subresource.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CLUSTER_ROLE=agent
CLUSTER_JOIN_ADDRESS=https://192.168.1.200:6443
CLUSTER_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/rancher/k3s/agent-join-token
NODE_TAINTS=
CHURN_KUBECONFIG_FILE=/etc/rancher/k3s/node-status-kubeconfig
# 5 -> 3 on 2026-11-09 (ci-runtime-and-cost, refined hp trigger on
# livespec-dev-tooling-6e5pyj): after the gate lane moved hp last, hp's jobs
# still ran 4.1-5.6x gmktec's and did not fall. The cohort drops 30 -> 28 and
# livespec-dev-tooling-cq donates the 2 slots. See ../../phase2/kueue/DERIVATION.md
# "hp to 3 slots, cohort 28 (2026-11-09)". Revert: this line back to 5, with
# dev-tooling-cq 9 and maxRunners 18.
ADMISSION_CAPACITY_C=3
